# CSE Builder Handoff

System: `{{ values.system.name }}`

Release: `{{ values.release }}`

This complete directory is the build handoff. It already contains the static
catalog snapshot, generated configuration scopes, all eight Spack environments,
deployment paths, module policy, and any lockfiles created so far.

There is one shared workspace for this system and release, not one workspace
per builder. The operator initializes it once and gives the receiving builder
this directory's absolute path. The receiving builder does not run
`init-workspace` and does not copy the workspace into a home directory. If this
directory or `./cse-build` does not exist, the handoff is not ready and the
operator must finish workspace initialization first.

The receiving builder needs membership in `{{ values.permissions.group }}` and
the site-provided absolute writable `WORKDIR`. Do not activate Spack or source
an operator-session file before running the entry point.

From the workspace root, run:

```bash
./cse-build
```

No system, release, compiler, MPI, catalog, install-tree, cache, view, module,
or environment values need to be entered. The command reads the values rendered
into this workspace. It selects or provisions the exact approved Spack runtime,
creates private state for the current builder, activates Spack and this
workspace, reports the current lock checkpoint, and creates or reattaches a
tmux session named for this system and release. Run the same command again after
a disconnect to return to that session.

For an explicit handoff to the recorded shared Spack checkout, use this first
entry sequence:

```bash
: "${WORKDIR:?WORKDIR must be set by the site environment}"
cd "<absolute-shared-workspace-path>"
test -r workspace-manifest.yaml
test -x ./cse-build
./cse-build status --spack-mode shared
```

If all eight lockfiles exist, run
`./cse-build verify --spack-mode shared`. If any lock is missing, run
`./cse-build concretize --spack-mode shared`; that action creates only missing
locks and verifies the complete set. Then run
`./cse-build --spack-mode shared` to create or reattach the prepared build
session. On later logins, return to the same absolute path and run the last
command again.

To open the prepared shell without tmux, run:

```bash
./cse-build shell
```

If tmux is not installed, the default command reports that fact and opens the
prepared shell directly.

The recorded Spack runtime is:

- source: `{{ values.spack.source }}`
- version: `{{ values.spack.version }}`
- tag: `{{ values.spack.tag }}`
- commit: `{{ values.spack.commit }}`
- preferred mode: `{{ values.spack.default_mode }}`
- shared root: `{{ values.spack.shared_root }}`
- builder-local root: `$HOME/STACK_TESTING/spack/{{ values.spack.version }}`

With no option, `cse-build` uses the recorded checkout when it is accessible.
Otherwise it follows the recorded shared/local preference. If the shared path
cannot be provisioned by this builder, automatic mode provisions the local
checkout instead. To choose explicitly:

```bash
./cse-build --spack-mode shared
./cse-build --spack-mode local
```

Actions launched from the prepared session retain that session's selected
Spack mode automatically.

The workspace may be handed over before concretization, after all lockfiles are
created, or partway through installation. These actions resume from the state
already present:

```bash
./cse-build status
./cse-build concretize
./cse-build verify
./cse-build fetch
./cse-build install
```

`concretize` creates only missing lockfiles. It does not replace existing
locks. `install` requires all locks and verifies them before building. Already
installed concrete hashes are reused from the shared restricted Spack store.
Each action starts by unloading a selected external compiler or MPI module when
that exact module is already active in the wrapper process. This lets Spack
1.2.2 activate the recorded module itself and avoids inheriting a conflicting
platform toolchain. Unrelated site modules and the caller's parent shell are
unchanged.

The environment is the build execution unit. `cse-build` is a convenience
entry point for preparing the shell and running common loops; it is not the
build scheduler. `./cse-build install` processes all eight environments
sequentially. If all locks have passed `./cse-build verify` and the real shared
install tree has passed the cross-node prefix-lock test, the operator may split
the two compiler surfaces across two build nodes:

```bash
# Node 1: shared GCC surface, sequential within this surface
./cse-build install --surface shared

# Node 2: platform-compiler surface, sequential within this surface
./cse-build install --surface platform
```

Both commands use the same locked workspace and restricted install tree.
Spack's prefix/database locks coordinate identical concrete hashes; the
surface split keeps view, module refresh, and mutable per-user cache roots
disjoint. The source/misc caches and package store remain shared. Do not run
two commands for the same surface. `BUILD_JOBS` is a per-process budget, so
two processes on one node would add their budgets together. `concretize` and
`verify` remain whole-workspace gates and do not accept `--surface`.

The operator may instead launch any distinct environment directly, on one node
or across several nodes. Open a prepared shell for each process:

```bash
./cse-build shell

environment="$SHARED_COMPILER_NAME/mpi-$SHARED_MPI_NAME"
environment_key="${environment//\//-}"
export SPACK_USER_CACHE_PATH="$SPACK_USER_STATE_ROOT/cache/$environment_key"
install -d -m 0700 "$SPACK_USER_CACHE_PATH"

spack -e "$CSE_BUILD_WORKSPACE/environments/$environment" \
  install --only-concrete -j "$BUILD_JOBS" --fail-fast
spack -e "$CSE_BUILD_WORKSPACE/environments/$environment" \
  env view regenerate
spack -e "$CSE_BUILD_WORKSPACE/environments/$environment" \
  module tcl refresh --delete-tree -y
```

Change `environment` for each process. The four GCC environments may run on
one node while the four platform environments run on another, or the operator
may choose a different distribution. Different hashes build concurrently. If
two processes reach the same exact hash, Spack's shared prefix/database lock
allows one to build while the other waits and then reuses the installed
prefix. Do not launch the same environment twice because its view and module
root are owned by that environment process. `BUILD_JOBS` is per process, so
size the sum of all process budgets on a node for its CPU and memory allocation.

## Commands used by the wrapper

`cse-build` is a thin Bash wrapper. For each applicable generated environment,
its actions run these Spack commands:

```bash
# status
spack -e <environment> find -lv

# concretize, only when spack.lock is absent
spack -e <environment> concretize --fresh -j 1

# configuration and lock verification
spack config scopes -vp
spack -e <environment> config scopes -vp
spack python scripts/verify-lockfiles.py

# login-node source fetch
spack -e <environment> fetch -D

# build and exposure refresh; sequential only when invoked by the wrapper loop
spack -e <environment> install --only-concrete -j "$BUILD_JOBS" --fail-fast
spack -e <environment> env view regenerate
spack -e <environment> module tcl refresh --delete-tree -y
```

The default session uses `tmux new-session -A` with the generated
`cse-{{ values.system.name }}-{{ values.release }}` session name. Inspect
`./cse-build` at any time; it is generated plain Bash, not a compiled build
engine.

Coordinate environment assignments whenever more than one process or builder
is active. Never launch the same environment twice. Concurrent work is allowed
only under the verified-lock conditions above. Before handing the workspace
back, record the last action, node, environment, result, and relevant log or
evidence path.
